---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
import { beeps } from '@/data/beeps';
import { workouts } from '@/data/workouts';
---

<TimerPage
  title="Interval Timer – Free Work, Rest and Rounds Timer"
  description="Set work, rest and rounds on a free interval timer. Check your session total, follow clear phase cues and find ready-made timings for shorter intervals."
  path="/interval"
  h1="Interval timer"
  lede="This interval timer alternates work and rest for up to 99 rounds. Set a schedule, check its total, and follow the phase cues with the page visible."
  config={defaultConfigs.interval}
  faq={faqs['/interval']}
  related={relatedTimers['/interval']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/interval']}
  crumbName="Interval timer"
>
  <Section title="Why use intervals?" flush>
    <p>
      Intervals divide an activity into repeated periods. In a run/walk session, one period is
      running and the next is walking. In a workout, the recovery is part of the plan, giving you
      time before the next effort.
    </p>
    <p>
      This structure makes the work and recovery explicit. It does not require every effort to be
      intense. Read <a href="/blog/hiit-interval-timer-beginners">how interval training works</a>{' '}
      for the distinction between a timing pattern and HIIT, or{' '}
      <a href="/blog/interval-timer-for-running">how run/walk training uses intervals</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Choose work, rest and rounds">
    <p>
      Work and Rest use whole minutes on this page. Rounds is the number of times the pair repeats.
      For example, Work 3, Rest 1 and Rounds 8 take 32 minutes. That includes one minute of recovery
      after the last work period.
    </p>
    <p>
      The default is 30 minutes of work and 5 minutes of rest for eight rounds: 280 minutes
      altogether. Change those values to match your plan before starting. For durations such as 30
      or 90 seconds, use an editable preset below.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="One block or repeating reminders">
    <p>
      Set Rest to 0 when you want consecutive work blocks without a break phase. Work 10 and Rounds
      6 then give you one hour, with boundaries every ten minutes and a finish cue at the end. Set
      Rounds to 1 for one timed block.
    </p>
    <p>
      For fixed starts with recovery inside each block, try <a href="/emom">EMOM</a>. For a focus
      cycle with short breaks and a final long break, use <a href="/pomodoro">Pomodoro</a>. The{' '}
      <a href="/blog/hiit-interval-timer-beginners">work and recovery guide</a> explains what
      changes when you adjust the ratio.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Before you press Start">
    <p>
      Start begins the first work period immediately. Test your volume and get ready first. A rising
      tone marks work, a falling tone marks rest, and warning ticks occur in the final three seconds
      of a phase.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep the page visible when cues matter. The timer requests an awake screen, but the browser
      can refuse it. If the page is suspended, the countdown can catch up when it resumes; sound may
      arrive late or be missed. Pause holds your place, and End session asks before ending a running
      or paused session.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Ready-made timers">
    <p>These pages open with a specific schedule and let you edit Work and Rest in seconds.</p>
    <ul class="links">
      {workouts.map((w) => (
        <li>
          <a href={w.path}>{w.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
      {beeps.map((b) => (
        <li>
          <a href={b.path}>{b.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
